WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:01.079 --> 00:00:05.830
Before we continue, I want to let you know
that for future print statements, I am not

00:00:05.830 --> 00:00:08.670
going to verbalize every character I am typing.

00:00:08.670 --> 00:00:12.179
I will call out what I am typing and then
type it and let you look over what has been

00:00:12.179 --> 00:00:14.180
added to the code.

00:00:14.180 --> 00:00:19.680
Whereas indexing extracts a value from a list,
slicing extracts characters from a list, word,

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:21.449
or phrase.

00:00:21.449 --> 00:00:29.869
For our slicing examples, I have the 123-slicing
file open, and we have, on line 1, a variable

00:00:29.869 --> 00:00:36.300
called serialnumber, a series of sixteen numbers,
with dashes between every four numbers.

00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:41.410
Each set of four numbers has some significance,
so we will see how to extract the first four

00:00:41.410 --> 00:00:48.160
numbers, the eight numbers in the middle with
the dash, and the last four numbers.

00:00:48.160 --> 00:00:52.760
Games may contain codes that have some of
the characters mean something, like a player

00:00:52.760 --> 00:00:57.370
status or something similar, so knowing how
to slice a value will save one from having

00:00:57.370 --> 00:01:02.399
to do it elsewhere, like in Excel, thus saving
time and potential chance for errors within

00:01:02.399 --> 00:01:04.299
an app.

00:01:04.299 --> 00:01:08.729
On line 2, I will add a print function to
output the first four characters from the

00:01:08.729 --> 00:01:12.190
serial_number variable.

00:01:12.190 --> 00:01:17.189
The slice is contained after the value and
in square brackets.

00:01:17.189 --> 00:01:25.210
Then, the start and end characters are separated
with a colon, though only a start or end needs

00:01:25.210 --> 00:01:26.829
to be specified.

00:01:26.829 --> 00:01:36.560
Thus, I will type a colon and then 4 to represent
that I want a slice starting at the beginning

00:01:36.560 --> 00:01:40.369
of the string and then continuing for four
characters.

00:01:40.369 --> 00:01:47.210
When I run this code, we get the first four
serial number characters.

00:01:47.210 --> 00:01:53.220
If you think a colon and 4 give us the first
four characters, what will happen if we reverse

00:01:53.220 --> 00:01:57.020
the slice and start with a 4 and then a colon?

00:01:57.020 --> 00:01:58.860
Let’s find out.

00:01:58.860 --> 00:02:08.950
I will copy the print statement on line two
to line three, make that change, and then

00:02:08.950 --> 00:02:11.430
run the code again.

00:02:11.430 --> 00:02:15.269
Did you expect to see this result?

00:02:15.269 --> 00:02:20.080
You may have thought that maybe you want to
see the last four characters, but this slice

00:02:20.080 --> 00:02:23.020
starts after character four.

00:02:23.020 --> 00:02:26.610
Let me repeat the key word: after.

00:02:26.610 --> 00:02:34.720
Remember that Python is zero-based, so technically,
we are beginning the slice on the fifth character,

00:02:34.720 --> 00:02:36.110
the first dash.

00:02:36.110 --> 00:02:40.910
And with nothing after the colon, we are telling
the slice to slice everything to the end of

00:02:40.910 --> 00:02:41.549
the value.

